nd direction against height, using the seven measurements to estimate a base direction and a veer rate. The regression gives roughly 36 degrees at the reference height and a veer of about -25 degrees per 100 m, with the low-altitude readings running a few degrees high.\n\nI'm trying to fit the wind speed against height now, and the readings look noisy. Taking a base speed of about 6.2 with a small shear exponent, the low-altitude values come out a bit high, so I'm trying a lower base speed and a shear around 0.2 to see if that fits better.\n\nI'm settling on a wind speed of 6.0 with a shear exponent of 0.25, and keeping the direction at 36 degrees with a veer of -25 per 100 m and a drag coefficient of 0.027."}, {"before_call": 19, "text": "I'm checking the residuals, which look like they're within noise, though the x-direction shows a slight positive bias. That suggests the predicted direction is a bit too far east, so I'm considering a small counterclockwise adjustment to the wind direction, from 36\u00b0 toward 34\u00b0 or 38\u00b0, and working out whether that reduces the x component.\n\nI'm comparing the residuals and settling on a few wind direction and speed settings to try next, with wind direction at 34 and wind speed at 5.9."}, {"before_call": 20, "text": "I've settled on the test setup, so the three remaining drops go to refining the updraft. Smoke is what matters here: the updraft strength varies with target intensity, peaking around 8 m/s, and it shifts the fall time by roughly a second, which means several meters of drift. The load also passes through the column horizontally, so I want a couple of smoke drops at different altitudes to pin that down.\n\nI'm planning two smoke drops to check the updraft at different heights: one at 150 m aimed at the center, one at 40 m, and I'll use simulate to set the speeds and aim before I run them."}, {"before_call": 22, "text": "I'm testing the first drop: released at the origin with heading 200 and altitude 150, it